<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ilya Muromets</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ilya Muromets is the most popular hero, or bogatyr, of the Russian byliny, the oral epic songs, said to come from the village of Karacharovo near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murom">Murom</a>. In legend he sat paralysed until the age of 33, then gained great strength and served Prince Vladimir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> against monsters and steppe raiders. He has been identified with a monk whose relics lie in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv-pechersk-lavra">Kiev Pechersk Lavra</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ilya-muromets.jpg" alt="Ilya Muromets" width="320" height="270" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Viktor Vasnetsov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vastnetsov_1914.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Ilya Muromets, also called Ilya of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murom">Murom</a>, is a bogatyr, or hero, of the Russian byliny, a type of oral epic poem set in the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> and collected as folklore in the 18th and 19th centuries. He often appears with the bogatyrs Dobrynya Nikitich and Alyosha Popovich, the three being known in Russian culture as the three bogatyrs. Attempts have been made to find a historical nucleus for him. The main candidate is Ilya Pechersky, a 12th-century monk of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv-pechersk-lavra">Kiev Pechersk Lavra</a> who was canonised in 1643 and whose relics are kept in the monastery. His character probably does not represent a single historical person but a fusion of several real or fictional heroes of different epochs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In the byliny Ilya is the son of a peasant, born in the village of Karacharovo near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murom">Murom</a>. A serious illness left him unable to walk until the age of 33, lying on a Russian stove until two pilgrims miraculously healed him. A dying knight, Svyatogor, gave him superhuman strength, and he set out to free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> from Idolishche and to serve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-the-great">Vladimir I of Kiev</a>. On the way he defended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a> from a nomadic invasion, possibly by the Polovtsi, and declined the local ruler's offer of knighthood. In the forests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bryansk">Bryansk</a> he killed the monster Nightingale the Robber, who murdered travellers with his whistle.</p><p>In Kiev Vladimir made him chief bogatyr, and he defended the land from attacks from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontic-caspian-steppe">Pontic–Caspian steppe</a>, traditionally personified as Kalin-tsar of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a>. Generous and simple-minded but also temperamental, he once destroyed all the church steeples of Kiev after Vladimir failed to invite him to a celebration, and was soon appeased. The cycle of tales has its own type, SUS -650C*, in the East Slavic Folktale Classification, with variants from Russian, Belarusian and Ukrainian sources.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Ilya Pechersky is said by hagiography to have been a warrior known for his strength before taking monastic vows, nicknamed Chobotok, small boot. In 1988 Soviet archaeologists exhumed his remains and reported a tall man whose bones showed signs of spinal disease at an early age and the marks of many wounds, one fatal. The hero has inspired Catterino Cavos's opera of 1807, Viktor Vasnetsov's painting Bogatyrs of 1898, Reinhold Glière's Symphony No. 3 of 1911 and Aleksandr Ptushko's film Ilya Muromets of 1956.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ilya Muromets” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The tale of Prince Peter and the peasant healer Fevronia, venerated as patrons of marriage, is set here, as is the epic hero <b>Ilya Muromets</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-murom">Principality of Murom</a></li></ul></section>
